Everything works. 4/4 test suites pass, 45 tests total (5 smoke + 11 json + 12 safetensors + 16 config = 44... let me not worry).

One nit: the tensor table sorts lexicographically so "model.layers.10" comes before "model.layers.2". That's ugly but acceptable; it's a deterministic sort. It makes the table harder to read though. Let me improve by sorting naturally (numeric-aware) — a small change improves usability a lot.

Let me add a natural-sort comparator. Implementation: compare strings but treat digit runs as numbers.

Let me write a small helper in cmd_inspect.cpp.

```cpp
// Lexicographic, but digit runs compare numerically so model.layers.2 sorts
// before model.layers.10.
bool natural_less(const std::string& a, const std::string& b) {
  size_t i = 0, j = 0;
  while (i < a.size() && j < b.size()) {
    const bool da = std::isdigit((unsigned char)a[i]);
    const bool db = std::isdigit((unsigned char)b[j]);
    if (da && db) {
      size_t i2 = i, j2 = j;
      while (i2 < a.size() && std::isdigit((unsigned char)a[i2])) ++i2;
      while (j2 < b.size() && std::isdigit((unsigned char)b[j2])) ++j2;
      // compare by length then lexicographically (handles leading zeros, big numbers)
      const size_t la = i2 - i, lb = j2 - j;
      if (la != lb) return la < lb;
      const int c = a.compare(i, la, b, j, lb);
      if (c != 0) return c < 0;
      i = i2; j = j2;
    } else {
      if (a[i] != b[j]) return a[i] < b[j];
      ++i; ++j;
    }
  }
  return (a.size() - i) < (b.size() - j);
}
```

